  "body": "## Description:\n\nThe BME680 gas sensor reports only an ohmic resistance, not a CO2 concentration, so derive an indoor air quality index from the gas resistance instead. The scale follows the Bosch BSEC convention of 0 (clean air) to 500 (heavily polluted air) documented in the BME680 datasheet, Sec. 4.2 and Table 4\n(https://www.bosch-sensortec.com/media/boschsensortec/downloads/datasheets/bst-bme680-ds001.pdf).\n\nThe arithmetic is an adaptation of the basic open-source IAQ calculator in thstielow/raspi-bme680-iaq (https://github.com/thstielow/raspi-bme680-iaq), which circumvents the closed-source BSEC library by squaring the ratio between the gas resistance and a baseline ceiling value for a steeper slope at high air quality and rescaling the result to a 0-500 index. The percentage-based gas quality and the baseline/drift handling are inspired by the example in the Pimoroni bme680-python library (https://github.com/pimoroni/bme680-python). In place of the linear rescale IAQ = (1 - AQ/100) * 500 used there, this driver maps the squared excess ratio through a saturating Hill function u = (k * x)^2 with IAQ = 500 * u / (1 + u), where x = R_baseline / R_gas - 1 and resistance below the baseline is clamped to zero. A tVOC equivalent in ppb is derived from the same excess ratio with a saturating (Michaelis-Menten style) curve.\n\nThe metal-oxide resistance also depends on ambient humidity, so the reading is normalized to a dry-air equivalent before it enters the baseline and the ratio. As in the thstielow reference, absolute humidity is derived from temperature and relative humidity with the Magnus equation and the ideal gas law, and the resistance is scaled by exp(ph_slope * hum_abs), with ph_slope defaulting to 0.03 per g/m3. This keeps the index stable across humidity changes instead of drifting with them.\n\nThe baseline is the clean-air ceiling of that compensated resistance. It is learned as a running mean during an initial burn-in of 2880 measurements, about 96 minutes at the 2 second reading interval, and then adapted in two directions: towards cleaner readings with an exponential moving average of 0.005, a time constant of about 7 minutes, and back down with a time constant of about 2.3 days to follow the slow aging of the metal-oxide layer. Only the clean-air direction moves the ceiling quickly, so a pollution event does not drag the reference along with it and flatten the index.\n\nReadings outside 1..5000 kOhm are dropped from the index, which rejects individual sensor glitches while leaving clean, dry air well inside the range, and a stored baseline is validated against the wider range that the humidity compensation can produce.\n\nMQTT and the JSON API report the raw 0-500 index and the tVOC value in ppb. The WebUI shows the same information as a 0-100 quality percentage where 100 is clean air, plus the tVOC in ppb, both with localized labels from the language files. While the burn-in runs, the WebUI reports the calibration progress instead, and no index is published until it is based on a real baseline.\n\nThe console command BmpIaq reports, sets and resets the gas baseline:\n\n*  `BmpIaq`                      prints the current baseline as the uncompensated resistance in kOhm, directly comparable with the                                      Gas reading\n*  `BmpIaq <1..5000>`    stores the value as the calibrated clean-air  baseline in kOhm, normalized to dry air for the                                       ratio; values outside 1..5000 kOhm are rejected\n*  `BmpIaq Reset`           discards the learned baseline and restarts the burn-in\n\n\nThe baseline is written to flash as soon as the burn-in completes and again on a graceful restart or OTA update through the save-before-restart hook. It is kept in a free area of the reserved TSettings block on every CPU target, so the calibration survives reboots and updates without frequent flash writes.\n\n\n<img width=\"393\" height=\"346\" alt=\"Bildschirmfoto vom 2026-10-22 17-14-35\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/2e2eb678-dc58-4009-bec2-737a06ad0d68\" />\n\n**Related issue (if applicable):** fixes #<Tasmota issue number goes here>\n\n## Checklist:\n  - [x] The pull request is done against the latest development branch\n  - [x] Only relevant files were touched\n  - [x] Only one feature/fix was added per PR and the code change compiles without warnings\n  - [x] The code change is tested and works with Tasmota core ESP8266 V.2.7.8\n  - [ ] The code change is tested and works with Tasmota core ESP32 V.3.3.12 from Platform 2026.09.50\n  - [x] I accept the [CLA](https://github.com/arendst/Tasmota/blob/development/CONTRIBUTING.md#contributor-license-agreement-cla).\n\n_NOTE: The code change must pass CI tests. **Your PR cannot be merged unless tests pass**_\n",
